Pipeline Flow
The tsbindgen pipeline transforms .NET assemblies through six distinct phases.
Phase 1: Load
File: Load/AssemblyLoader.cs
Loads .NET assemblies using MetadataLoadContext and reflects all type information.
Input: Assembly file paths
Output: Initial SymbolGraph with raw CLR data
Operations:
Create MetadataLoadContext for isolated assembly inspection
Load each assembly from disk
Reflect all public types and their members
Build TypeSymbol for each type
Create NamespaceSymbol groupings
Phase 2: Model (Index Building)
File: Builder.cs (inline)
Builds indices for fast type lookup and applies library mode filtering.
Operations:
Build TypeIndex dictionary (CLR full name to TypeSymbol)
Apply --lib filtering (exclude types from library package)
Resolve forward references between types
Phase 3: Shape (23 Transformation Passes)
File: Shape/*.cs
Transforms the symbol graph for TypeScript emission. See shape.md for all passes.
Phase 3.5: Normalize
File: Normalize/NameReservation.cs
Reserves all TypeScript identifiers and resolves conflicts. See normalize.md.
Phase 4: Plan
File: Plan/*.cs
Plans all import/export statements and validates invariants. See plan.md.
Phase 5: Emit
File: Emit/*.cs
Generates TypeScript declaration files. See emit.md.
Pipeline Orchestration
File: Builder.cs
public static void Build(BuildContext ctx, GenerateOptions options)
{
    // Phase 1: Load
    var graph = AssemblyLoader.Load(ctx, options.Assemblies);

    // Phase 2: Model
    graph = BuildIndices(graph);

    // Phase 3: Shape (23 passes)
    graph = StructuralConformance.Analyze(ctx, graph);
    graph = ViewPlanner.Plan(ctx, graph);
    // ... more passes ...

    // Phase 3.5: Normalize
    NameReservation.ReserveAll(ctx, graph);

    // Phase 4: Plan
    var emissionPlan = EmissionPlanner.Plan(ctx, graph, ...);
    PhaseGate.Validate(ctx, emissionPlan);

    // Phase 5: Emit
    InternalIndexEmitter.Emit(ctx, emissionPlan, options.OutDir);
    FacadeEmitter.Emit(ctx, emissionPlan, options.OutDir);
    FamilyIndexEmitter.Emit(ctx, families, options.OutDir);  // families.json
}

Importing Modules
Preferred with surface: "@tsonic/js" and @tsonic/nodejs: Node-style specifiers
With surface: "@tsonic/js" and @tsonic/nodejs installed, import using Node module names:
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";
import * as process from "node:process";
Bare aliases are also supported:
import { readFileSync } from "fs";
